  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nA pair of waves move onto the Plains tonight. The first will be \nassociated with the right entrance region of the upper jet to the \nnorthwest. This may bring some some stronger storms to central SD \nlater this evening. An isolated severe storm will be possible. Later \ntonight a more subtropical wave moves from CO into northern NE and \nmay interact with a much more unstable environment. If this develops \nit will likely become linear with heavy rain and wind gusts to \naround 70 mph likely. The better chances for this coming together \nwill be near the Missouri River into northwest IA, likely from about \n2 am to 7 am. CAPE values likely running about 20000-4000 J/kg with \nmoderate deep layer shear. \n\nOnce this activity exits the area, fairly stable air settles in for \nmuch of Thursday into Thursday evening. Thursday morning may see some \nlow stratus and patchy fog, with these clouds lifting a bit but \nlikely lingering through the day. This should limit warming a bit \nwith highs likely remaining in the 70s. However with these cooler \ntemperatures will not come a drop in humidity with dew points still \nlikely in the mid 60s to lower 70s.\n\nLater Thursday night into Friday morning a weak wave will lift north \ninto the area and should bring weak to moderate warm air advection \nin the low to mid levels. With the potential for maybe 1000 J/kg \nCAPE a few thunderstorms will be possible, with the better chances in \nnortheast NE and northwest IA. Severe weather is unlikely but local \nheavy downpours are possible.\n\nFriday afternoon and Friday night weak upper level ridging will \nbuild and likely suppress any convective activity. However Saturday \ninto Saturday evening showers and thunderstorms will likely \nredevelop with very isolated severe storms and locally heavy rain \npossible.\n\nFaster northwest flow aloft will be north of the area Monday through \nWednesday next week. This will keep a slightly cool air mass in \nplace during this time with highs generally from 75 to 85 each day. \nPeriodic weak waves will move through and bring chances for showers \nand thunderstorms with low confidence on strength and timing right \nnow.",
